> Are stoners now[ a target advertising demographic] for fast food companies?
Ever since Harold and Kumar went to White Castle, fast-food companies have[ become] increasingly blatant about targeting to stoners in their advertisements.
July 31, 2012, 1:48 a.m.
Between Taco Bell's "Fourthmeal" advertisements and Jack in the Box commercials featuring slow-talking stoners,[ it]'s hardly a secret anymore that fast-food companies are[ shameless]ly targeting potheads with their[ ads].[ But] have they crossed a line?
Once considered a taboo marketing approach,[ the fast]-[food industry] today[ appears] to have brazenly embraced[ the] late-[night] munch[ies], according to The Fix. Although[ advertisements] never blatantly refer to pot smoking, subliminal messaging has become more obvious.
Take, for instance, this recent[ Jack] in the Box advertisement. And Taco Bell[ has] created a jingle to go with its latest string of advertisements[,] which sings of the "late-[night] munchies." Of course, drive-thru windows open well into the morning hours also cater to[ the] average[ midnight] sn[acker], but there's little denying who[ the real] target is given[ the] choice of[ terminology].
